    title: 'Identity Development and Social Identity Theory',
    passageText: `Erik Erikson proposed that psychosocial development occurs in eight stages across the lifespan, each characterized by a central conflict whose resolution contributes to identity formation. The fifth stage — identity vs. role confusion — occurs during adolescence and represents a critical period for developing a coherent sense of self. Successful resolution produces a stable identity; failure results in role confusion and difficulties with commitment. James Marcia extended Erikson's framework, identifying four identity statuses based on the dimensions of exploration (active searching) and commitment (adopting a stance): identity diffusion (no exploration, no commitment), foreclosure (commitment without exploration), moratorium (exploration without commitment), and identity achievement (exploration followed by commitment).

Henri Tajfel and John Turner's Social Identity Theory (SIT) addresses identity from a sociological rather than developmental perspective. SIT holds that individuals categorize themselves and others into social groups and derive part of their identity from group membership. Social categorization, social identification (internalizing group membership), and social comparison (evaluating one's group relative to out-groups) together produce in-group favoritism and out-group discrimination. According to SIT, individuals are motivated to maintain positive social identity, leading to biases in perception and behavior when the in-group is perceived as inferior.

Researchers studying undergraduate political identity found that students who identified strongly with a political party showed more extreme in-group favoritism (positive ratings of their party) and out-group derogation (negative ratings of the opposing party) than students with weak party identification. Self-esteem moderated this relationship: students with lower self-esteem showed stronger in-group favoritism, consistent with SIT's prediction that social identity compensates for personal identity deficits.`,
