    title: 'Social Influence and Group Dynamics',
    passageText: `Solomon Asch's conformity experiments (1951) demonstrated that individuals often conform to a unanimous but incorrect majority. In Asch's paradigm, confederates stated obviously wrong answers to simple line-length comparison tasks; naive participants conformed to the incorrect majority answer approximately 37% of the time, despite knowing the correct answer. Conformity rates decreased when any dissent was present — even a single person disagreeing with the majority substantially reduced conformity.

Social psychologists distinguish between informational social influence (conforming because we believe others have information we lack — "I don't know, so I'll follow the group") and normative social influence (conforming to be liked or to avoid social rejection, even when the person privately disagrees). Informational influence is more likely in ambiguous situations; normative influence is more likely when group approval is important. Moscovici's work on minority influence demonstrated that small, consistent minorities can shift majority opinion over time, particularly when the minority is seen as credible and confident.

Groupthink (Janis, 1982) occurs when the desire for group cohesion and unanimity overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives. Symptoms include illusion of invulnerability, collective rationalization, stereotyping of out-groups, and self-censorship of dissenting opinions. Conditions promoting groupthink: high group cohesiveness, isolation from outside opinion, directive leadership, and homogeneous membership. Classic examples include the Bay of Pigs invasion and the Challenger launch decision.

Deindividuation occurs when individuals in groups lose their sense of individual identity and responsibility, often resulting in behavior that departs from personal norms. Philip Zimbardo's studies (including the Stanford Prison Experiment) examined how situational forces — uniform, anonymity, role assignment — can lead individuals to behave in ways inconsistent with their personal values.`,
