    title: 'Emotion and Emotional Processing',
    passageText: `Emotions are multidimensional experiences involving physiological arousal, subjective experience, cognitive appraisal, and expressive behavior. Several theories have been proposed to explain the relationship between these components. The James-Lange theory holds that emotion follows the perception of physiological changes: we feel afraid because our heart races, not the reverse. The Cannon-Bard theory challenged this, arguing that physiological arousal and subjective experience occur simultaneously and independently — thalamic signals reach both the cortex (subjective experience) and the periphery (arousal) at the same time. The Schachter-Singer two-factor theory proposes that emotion arises from two components: physiological arousal (which is non-specific — the same autonomic arousal underlies different emotions) and cognitive labeling (which gives the arousal its emotional meaning, based on contextual cues).

The facial feedback hypothesis holds that facial expressions do not merely reflect emotional experience but can influence it. When individuals produce a smile (either naturally or by holding a pen between their teeth, which mimics the smiling musculature), they rate stimuli as funnier and more pleasant than when making no expression or a frowning expression. This suggests that proprioceptive feedback from facial muscles contributes to subjective emotional experience.

Basic emotions research, associated with Paul Ekman, identified six primary emotions (happiness, sadness, anger, fear, disgust, surprise) whose facial expressions are recognized cross-culturally, suggesting a biological substrate for at least some emotional displays. Critics argue that emotional expression is substantially modulated by cultural display rules — norms about which emotions are appropriate to express in which contexts.

Emotional regulation strategies include: cognitive reappraisal (changing the meaning or interpretation of a situation before it produces an emotional response), expressive suppression (inhibiting outward emotional expression while internal arousal continues), and mindfulness (non-judgmental attention to emotional experience). Reappraisal is generally considered more adaptive than suppression — suppression requires ongoing effort and does not reduce subjective distress.',`,
