    title: 'Health Behavior Models',
    passageText: `Understanding why individuals engage or fail to engage in health-protective behaviors is central to public health intervention design. Several theoretical models have been developed to predict and change health behavior.

The Health Belief Model (HBM) holds that health behavior is determined by the individual's perceptions of: (1) perceived susceptibility (likelihood of contracting the health condition); (2) perceived severity (seriousness of the condition); (3) perceived benefits (effectiveness of the protective behavior); and (4) perceived barriers (costs, inconveniences, side effects). Cues to action (reminders, symptoms, media) trigger behavior change. Self-efficacy — the belief in one's ability to perform the behavior — was added as a fifth component. Health behaviors are adopted when perceived benefits outweigh perceived barriers.

The Transtheoretical Model (TTM) of behavior change (Prochaska and DiClemente) describes change as progressing through stages: precontemplation (not considering change), contemplation (considering change but not committed), preparation (planning to act soon), action (actively changing behavior, < 6 months), and maintenance (sustaining change > 6 months). Decisional balance (weighing pros vs. cons) shifts across stages: in early stages, cons outweigh pros; this reverses in action stages. Relapse is common and seen as part of the change process, not as failure.

Social cognitive theory (Bandura) emphasizes that behavior is influenced by: self-efficacy (belief in one's ability to perform a specific behavior — the strongest predictor of behavior change), outcome expectations (beliefs about the results of the behavior), and observational learning (learning from modeling). Self-efficacy is enhanced through: mastery experiences (direct success), vicarious learning (observing others succeed), verbal persuasion (encouragement), and physiological states (interpreting arousal as readiness rather than anxiety).`,
